speechtext:
the second reading of Bill No. S-18, to incorporate Lutheran Church-Canada.
He said: As sponsor of this measure it is my privilege to provide information to this house and reasons why, in my submission, favourable consideration should be given to its passage. This bill seeks to give corporate status to the members of this Lutheran church in Canada who are adherents to the Missouri synod. In the early years, and up to the present time, members of this church have been organized into three districts, namely, Ontario, Manitoba-Saskatche-wan, and Alberta-British Columbia. These three bodies have done the preliminary work with a view to becoming one body, and are now seeking incorporation. In addition to those belonging to the three groups I have mentioned, there are others who have, because of proximity to other Lutheran churches in the United States, been affiliated in the United States districts. These will also be included in the body now being incorporated.
At the present time this church is affiliated with the Missouri synod of the church in the United States, but preparations are now being made for the establishment of an independent Canadian body in the future. The church has congregations in the provinces of Quebec, Ontario, Manitoba, Saskatchewan, Alberta and British Columbia. Television and radio coverage is provided in all the provinces. At present there are about 333 congregations and 76,000 baptized members.
Mr. Speaker, the object of the corporation shall be:

(a)	to promote, maintain, superintend and carry on in accordance with the faith, doctrines, constitution, acts and rulings of the corporation any or all of the work of that body;
(b)	to advance and increase the diffusion of the faith of the corporation in all lawful ways;
(c)	to organize, establish, maintain and carry on residences, missions, churches, places of worship, parsonages, orphanages, homes for the aged, rest homes and institutions and agencies for promoting, teaching, propagating and disseminating the Lutheran faith and doctrine and for training persons for the said purposes;
(d)	to promote, organize, establish, maintain and carry on social service, welfare and guidance institutions and agencies;
(e)	to promote education, instruction and culture, and to organize, establish, maintain and carry on schools, colleges, academies, seminaries, institutions of learning, recreational halls, centers and agencies, and industrial, technical and agricultural institutes and farms;
(f)	to promote charity and to care for the poor, and to organize, establish, maintain and carry on charitable institutions, hospitals, clinics, dispensaries and cemeteries;
(g)	to organize, establish, maintain and carry on libraries and houses and agencies for printing, publishing and disseminating literature, news papers, periodicals and works of education, religion, art and science;
(h)	to promote the spiritual welfare of all the congregations and mission fields of the corporation.
As a member of this church, and on behalf of all its members I would appreciate the approval of this bill by the house.
